  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\n\nRain will continue at times through the day today before tapering \noff. Instability is limited and therefore while thunder cannot be \nruled out, expect thunderstorm activity to be isolated. Flooding or \nsevere weather is not expected with this system. \n\nAs the remnants of Hurricane Isaias move out of the area, fog will\nstart to develop Sunday night into Monday morning. There will be the\npotential that a dense fog advisory might be needed at some point. \nWestern portions of the region will have more of a potential for \ndense fog as skies will have a better chance to clear out. Added fog \nmention into the forecast and into the HWO.\n\nKey Message 2)\n\nDry conditions are expected during the day on Monday. In addition,\nTuesday is trending drier with precipitation chances and now\nprecipitation is expected to hold off until Tuesday night into \nWednesday. With the drier weather expected for Tuesday now, \ntemperatures are expected to be well above normal with high \ntemperatures in the 80s areawide. \n\nThere will be multiple additional chances for rain late in the week\nand into the weekend. There is still some uncertainty later in the \nweekend on how strong that system will be and the associated cold \nair. At this time however there is at least some potential for the \nfirst widespread frost later in the weekend with some freezing \ntemperatures also possible. Held off on mentioning this in the HWO \nuntil there is model consensus and confidence in the colder air.",
